<<<
ceremonial, ceremonious, aa. Ceremonial means connected with or
===
**ceremonial, ceremonious,** aa. *Ceremonial* means connected with or
>>>

<<<
for a ceremony (i.e. a piece of ritual or formality) or ceremonies (the -al | law; a-al occasion; for -al reasons; -al costume).
===
for a ceremony (*i.e. a piece of ritual or formality*) or ceremonies (*the -al law; a -al occasion; for -al reasons; -al costume*).
>>>

<<<
Ceremonious means | full of or resulting from ceremony i.e. attention to forms (why be so | -ous? ; -ous people; -ous politeness).
===
*Ceremonious* means full of or resulting from ceremony i.e. attention to forms (*why be so -ous? ; -ous people; -ous politeness*).
>>>

<<<
even with change of meaning. = But with some words -al & -ous are both possible, though not indifferent:
===
even with change of meaning. But with some words *-al* & *-ous* are both possible, though not indifferent:
>>>

<<<
a -ous court is a sovereign’s court in which ceremony is much observed; a -al court would be a judicial court set up to regulate ceremonies;
===
*a -ous court* is a sovereign's court in which ceremony is much observed; *a -al court* would be a judicial court set up to regulate ceremonies;
>>>

<<<
A visitor may make a -ous entry into a room, but an army a -al eniry into a town that has , capitulated.
===
A visitor may make *a -ous entry* into a room, but an army *a -al entry* into a town that has capitulated.
>>>

